WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:00.993
- Hey there, folks.

00:00:00.993 --> 00:00:04.443
Welcome to pro tips from pro chefs.

00:00:06.760 --> 00:00:08.540
I am Andrew Rea of Binging with Babish,

00:00:08.540 --> 00:00:11.970
decidedly not a pro chef, but
they gave me some airtime,

00:00:11.970 --> 00:00:13.370
I'm gonna use it to talk to you

00:00:13.370 --> 00:00:15.090
about salt and pepper.

00:00:15.090 --> 00:00:16.880
It sounds pretty boring, right?

00:00:16.880 --> 00:00:17.823
But it's not.

00:00:19.490 --> 00:00:21.360
There's actually all
different kinds of salt,

00:00:21.360 --> 00:00:22.800
and all different kinds of pepper,

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:24.630
but I'm gonna show you
the ones that you need

00:00:24.630 --> 00:00:26.130
the most in your home kitchen.

00:00:28.360 --> 00:00:29.900
First up, we have table salt.

00:00:29.900 --> 00:00:31.840
This is the salt that you're
probably most familiar with.

00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:33.460
It's the one that's on your dinner table.

00:00:33.460 --> 00:00:35.620
It's the one that comes in
these whimsical cardboard tubes,

00:00:35.620 --> 00:00:37.470
with the little metal spout
that always breaks off

00:00:37.470 --> 00:00:38.880
and ruins your day.

00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:42.360
See how the salt just is
cascading from my fingers?

00:00:42.360 --> 00:00:43.367
While it's very good for baking,

00:00:43.367 --> 00:00:46.610
not so good for cooking because
first off, as you can see,

00:00:46.610 --> 00:00:47.810
I can't pinch it.

00:00:47.810 --> 00:00:51.160
You see how I have no
control over what's happening

00:00:51.160 --> 00:00:52.430
below my fingernails?

00:00:52.430 --> 00:00:53.900
I feel out of control right now,

00:00:53.900 --> 00:00:56.520
not a man in control of my situation,

00:00:56.520 --> 00:00:58.323
my life, or anything really.

00:00:59.463 --> 00:01:00.940
(Andrew chuckles)

00:01:00.940 --> 00:01:02.890
Let's get rid of all this spilled salt.

00:01:05.630 --> 00:01:06.550
Bad luck.

00:01:06.550 --> 00:01:09.200
Okay, so, just so you guys know,

00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:11.890
we love keeping in the
flubs and the stupid stuff.

00:01:11.890 --> 00:01:15.450
So feel free to keep all the silliness in.

00:01:15.450 --> 00:01:16.670
Now, for cooking.

00:01:16.670 --> 00:01:18.130
As you cook more and more often,

00:01:18.130 --> 00:01:19.310
you wanna salt to taste.

00:01:19.310 --> 00:01:20.790
That's what most recipes say, right?

00:01:20.790 --> 00:01:21.890
Salt to taste.

00:01:21.890 --> 00:01:23.610
For that, you wanna go kosher salt.

00:01:23.610 --> 00:01:25.680
Kosher salt is the choice
for chefs the world over

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:27.270
because you can control it.

00:01:27.270 --> 00:01:29.820
You can pick it up, feel how
much you're grabbing here.

00:01:29.820 --> 00:01:32.340
This feels like about three
quarters of a teaspoon to me,

00:01:32.340 --> 00:01:33.280
if I had to guess.

00:01:33.280 --> 00:01:38.280
And then you can control how
much is going into your food,

00:01:38.820 --> 00:01:41.270
and you get used to that and
you can cook more fluidly.

00:01:41.270 --> 00:01:43.380
You can cook by salting to taste.

00:01:43.380 --> 00:01:45.180
That's what most recipes say, right?

00:01:46.040 --> 00:01:46.873
Yes.

00:01:47.970 --> 00:01:50.390
So the last salt that I want
you to keep in your pantry

00:01:50.390 --> 00:01:51.950
is flaky finishing salt.

00:01:51.950 --> 00:01:54.530
Now these are big old
honking salt crystals.

00:01:54.530 --> 00:01:55.370
You see that?

00:01:55.370 --> 00:01:56.950
These are not only gonna
add a nice little bit

00:01:56.950 --> 00:01:59.600
of salty bite to your dish,

00:01:59.600 --> 00:02:01.450
it's also gonna add a
whole lot of texture.

00:02:01.450 --> 00:02:02.430
Also looks really nice.

00:02:02.430 --> 00:02:04.910
This looks delicious, and tastes delicious

00:02:04.910 --> 00:02:07.580
on brownies, on cookies,
on your salted caramel,

00:02:07.580 --> 00:02:10.220
on your steak when you
finished plating up the steak

00:02:10.220 --> 00:02:12.040
and slicing it, hitting
a little bit of butter

00:02:12.040 --> 00:02:15.110
and flaky salt, your guests
will never leave your home.

00:02:15.110 --> 00:02:19.585
You'll have to call a lawyer
about squatter's rights.

00:02:19.585 --> 00:02:20.418
(Andrew laughs)

00:02:20.418 --> 00:02:21.750
Didn't come to me fast enough.

00:02:22.770 --> 00:02:23.603
These are the salts.

00:02:23.603 --> 00:02:24.530
These are the salts that I want you

00:02:24.530 --> 00:02:26.040
to go out and buy right now.

00:02:26.040 --> 00:02:26.873
I'll wait.

00:02:32.080 --> 00:02:32.913
All right, so.

00:02:33.867 --> 00:02:35.190
All right, all right, all right.

00:02:35.190 --> 00:02:37.530
So let's get the salts out of the way.

00:02:37.530 --> 00:02:39.550
Let's get off all this
bad luck out of the way.

00:02:39.550 --> 00:02:40.610
Look at all this spilled salt.

00:02:40.610 --> 00:02:43.150
I'm gonna die tonight from this.

00:02:43.150 --> 00:02:47.780
The Babish bench scraper for
the discerning bench scraper.

00:02:47.780 --> 00:02:50.460
Like as a person, a person
who scrapes benches.

00:02:50.460 --> 00:02:51.330
A bench scraper.

00:02:51.330 --> 00:02:54.103
This is the bench scraper
for the one of them.

00:02:57.609 --> 00:03:00.000
(upbeat fun music)

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:01.820
So next up we're talking about pepper.

00:03:01.820 --> 00:03:03.520
So this is a little theme going on here.

00:03:03.520 --> 00:03:04.353
Salt and pepper.

00:03:04.353 --> 00:03:05.820
All right. So you got it.

00:03:05.820 --> 00:03:07.530
Pre-ground versus fresh ground.

00:03:07.530 --> 00:03:09.480
This is what most of us
have in our kitchens.

00:03:09.480 --> 00:03:12.300
The stuff in the shaker, the
stuff in the little square tin

00:03:12.300 --> 00:03:13.880
that has the pour a whole lot side,

00:03:13.880 --> 00:03:16.500
and the sprinkle a little bit side, right?

00:03:16.500 --> 00:03:18.120
No? Good for you.

00:03:18.120 --> 00:03:19.934
That must mean that you're
using freshly ground pepper,

00:03:19.934 --> 00:03:23.910
which I am a huge advocate
for, because it tastes bigger.

00:03:23.910 --> 00:03:24.790
It tastes brighter.

00:03:24.790 --> 00:03:26.230
It tastes more floral.

00:03:26.230 --> 00:03:28.084
You can control the grind size.

00:03:28.084 --> 00:03:29.280
That's about it.

00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:32.890
But all those things are worth
the cost of admission alone.

00:03:32.890 --> 00:03:34.000
And to prove it to you,

00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:36.950
I'm going to bring my
producer, Abby Glackin,

00:03:36.950 --> 00:03:38.280
right out in here.

00:03:38.280 --> 00:03:39.801
Is it pronounced Glackin or Glacken?

00:03:39.801 --> 00:03:40.634
- Glackin.

00:03:40.634 --> 00:03:41.530
- Okay. All right, cool.

00:03:41.530 --> 00:03:43.130
Now, I'm gonna mix these up.

00:03:43.130 --> 00:03:44.080
Close your eyes, please.

00:03:44.080 --> 00:03:45.580
Thank you. Keep them closed.

00:03:45.580 --> 00:03:47.520
And we have never met before, right?

00:03:47.520 --> 00:03:48.353
- Who are you?

00:03:51.270 --> 00:03:53.170
We have met before, that was the joke.

00:03:54.330 --> 00:03:55.790
So I'm gonna mix these up,

00:03:55.790 --> 00:03:57.670
and I'm gonna ask you to smell them,

00:03:57.670 --> 00:03:59.330
which is not a pleasant thing.

00:03:59.330 --> 00:04:00.870
It's never fun to smell pepper directly.

00:04:00.870 --> 00:04:01.990
So I apologize in advance,

00:04:01.990 --> 00:04:03.950
but you're gonna tell me
which one smells better.

00:04:03.950 --> 00:04:05.580
Big lift.

00:04:05.580 --> 00:04:06.860
- I mean, I don't even smell anything.

00:04:06.860 --> 00:04:08.094
- Okay, that's pepper A.

00:04:08.094 --> 00:04:08.927
- All right.

00:04:08.927 --> 00:04:10.423
- Now, what about pepper B?

00:04:11.710 --> 00:04:13.920
- Ooh, Ooh, Ooh.

00:04:13.920 --> 00:04:14.753
There's something there.

00:04:14.753 --> 00:04:15.586
- There's something there.

00:04:15.586 --> 00:04:16.750
See? All right.

00:04:16.750 --> 00:04:19.320
You want something there,
you want nothing there?

00:04:19.320 --> 00:04:20.610
You want freshly ground pepper.

00:04:20.610 --> 00:04:21.443
Thank you, Abby.

00:04:21.443 --> 00:04:22.760
Great to see you.
- Thank you.

00:04:24.870 --> 00:04:26.420
- Well folks, I hope
I managed to teach you

00:04:26.420 --> 00:04:27.750
something halfway interesting

00:04:27.750 --> 00:04:29.400
about a very uninteresting subject.

00:04:29.400 --> 00:04:30.930
Until next time, my name is Andrew Rea.

00:04:30.930 --> 00:04:32.300
Go check out my channel on YouTube.

00:04:32.300 --> 00:04:33.340
It's called Binging with Babish,

00:04:33.340 --> 00:04:35.540
and go to thedrewbarrymoreshow.com

00:04:35.540 --> 00:04:37.897
for more tips, tricks, and Drew.

